      "textEn": "27\n\nIn the praise, divine knowledge is in the seed mantra. The Mundaka Upanishad (1.2.12) states—\n\nFor the knowledge of that, one should approach a guru.\n\nOne who is self-controlled, who is a Srotriya (learner of Vedas), and who is devoted to Brahman. “To obtain accurate information about these matters, a person should take the Samidha in their hand and go to the Guru, who is proficient in the Vedas and has firm devotion to the ultimate truth.” And Katha\nThe Upanishad (2.9) declares—\n\nNaisha tarkayan matirapneya\n\nPrakatanyenaiva sujnanaya preshta. \"You darn old pudding! This feeling cannot be obtained by logic. A disciple capable of understanding this\nshould only be told by a very capable guru.\"\n\nOften, those who are following the disciple tradition towards a non-Vaishnava guru, they disregard the importance of devotion.\nInstead, the arrogant Yogi, relying on his own merit,\nand the wise, to establish their dominance over the world, display their own success from above,\nbut their fame is fleeting—\n\nFor those who are not devoted, the mind is engaged in pranayama and other practices. Akshayavasanam Rajan drishyate punarutthitam. “Those who engage their minds in practices like Abhakta Pranayama are not completely free from material desires. Therefore, O King! In their minds, material desires are seen to arise again.”\n( Bhagwat .51.60)\n\nOn the other hand, the humble, firm-determined devotee of Lord Vishnu and Vaishnavas easily\nachieves victory over the invincible mind. To stabilize the mind, one does not need to practice Ashtanga Yoga, nor do they need to adopt other means. All this is achieved by devotion to the Guru, by the man who conquers with effort—the only Guru—\nThrough devotion, a man can achieve all these goals. Otherwise, a non-devotee, his senses and"
