ी निश्चित न कर पाते कि हमारी अनुभूतियाँ तथ्यों के संगत हैं। हमारी दशा उस",
      "textEn": "3\n\nIt is about showing the definite separation between function and cause. Therefore, whatever arises from the truth, it will be\ndifferent from it in a meaningful way—that is, it will be momentary. In this way, the argument of materialists is flawed,\nbecause what needs to be proven is proven against it (tarkahatam), meaning this\nworld as we know it is only like that, it is eternal, and there is no divine truth separate from it. In the defense, the critics can claim that they do not want to prove the identity, but rather\nthey want to prove the possibility of difference, or in other words, the possibility of a truth different from the known world, wrong.\nThey want to prove it. This attempt to support the Mimamsa logic, from the term 'vyabhicharati kwa cha', is easily\neasily negated. The meaning of saying is that those who oppose the general rule,\nexamples are also found. Yes, sometimes the source is very different from the object produced by itself, such as\nman and his young son or the destruction of a hammer and a clay pot.\nBut the critics answer that the creation of the universe is not like your other\ncounter-examples: the father and the hammer are merely efficient causes, while the essence of this universe is also the material\ncause. This answer is hoped for by the words kwach mriṣa (sometimes the action is misleading). When there is an illusion of a snake in a rope on Earth, then the rope is the material cause of the illusion of a snake, which\ndiffers from the imaginary snake in many cases, especially due to being true. The Mimamsa then answers that the material cause of the imaginary serpent is not the rope itself—it is the rope\nthe ignorance (avidya) of the observer. Because ignorance is not an object, therefore, the serpent illusion arising from it is called. The direct Vedas answer that even after this, it is true that with ignorance, from truth,\n(tathobhayayuk) the universe is created. Here, the imaginary element of illusion, 