      "textEn": "35\n\nA person who, even after thorough investigation, doubts that a rope could be a snake. But\nNo. The scriptures here answer that the perishable form of matter is an illusion of the eternal spiritual truth,\nwhich is cleverly crafted to fulfill the desire of bound souls to perform physical actions.\n(Vyavahrutaye vikalp ishtah). The illusion of the stability of this world continues through the tradition of those blind men,\nwho learn materialistic ideas from their predecessors and transmit this illusion to their descendants. Anyone can see that even after removing the basis of illusion,\nunder the force of permanent mental influence, its mental impression remains. In this way, throughout history,\nblind philosophers have misled other blind people by assuring them that they can attain perfection by performing worldly\nrituals.\nFoolish people may exchange in counterfeit coins,\nbut a wise person knows that such wealth is useless for buying food, medicine, and other merchandise. If this net money is given in charity, then it will not bring any merit. But the Mimansak say that how can a devout person who performs Vedic rituals be deluded,\nbecause the Samhitas and Brahmins of the Vedic scriptures establish that the fruit of action\nis eternal? For example, Akshayam ha vai chaturmasyayajinah sukrutam bhavati—those who observe the Chaturmas Vrata receive inexhaustible good deeds, and apam somam amritam bhavim—we have drunk the nectar of Soma and\nhave become immortal (Rigveda 8.3.3). By indicating this, the scriptures answer that the intelligent words of God, from which the Vedas are made,\nbewitch them, whose weak intellect has been crushed by the burden of excessive faith in action.\nHere,\na specific word has been used, which indicates that Vedic mantras, in terms of secondary, allusory, and other linguistic-\nscientific meanings, by providing various kinds of misleading meanings, protect their secrets from all,\nexcept from those who have faith in Lord Vishnu. In their orders, the Vedas do not actually say that\nthe fruits of action are eternal, but indirectly praise regular sacrifices through metaphors.\nThey do. The Chandogya Upanishad definitely says that the fruits of ritual actions are temporary—Tad\nYatheh karmachito lokah kshiyate evam evamutra punyachito lokah kshiyate—In the same way, in this world, difficult"
